1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,660
Section 2

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:05,795
We'll be arriving at Branley Castle in about five minutes,

3
00:00:05,795 --> 00:00:10,150
but before we get there I'll give you a little information about the castle

4
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:13,020
and what our visit will include.

5
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:17,965
So in fact there's been a castle on this site for over eleven hundred

6
00:00:17,965 --> 00:00:18,970
years.

7
00:00:18,970 --> 00:00:25,572
The first building was a fort constructed in 914 AD for defence against Danish

8
00:00:25,572 --> 00:00:28,361
invaders by King Alfred the Great's daughter,

9
00:00:28,361 --> 00:00:31,090
who ruled England at the time.

10
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:36,141
In the following century, after the Normans conquered England, the land was given to

11
00:00:36,141 --> 00:00:38,446
a nobleman called Richard de Vere,

12
00:00:38,446 --> 00:00:42,695
and he built a castle there that stayed in the de Vere family for

13
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:45,000
over four hundred years.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,002
However,

15
00:00:46,002 --> 00:00:49,628
when Queen Elizabeth I announced that she was going to visit the castle in

16
00:00:49,628 --> 00:00:53,375
1576, it was beginning to look a bit run down,

17
00:00:53,375 --> 00:00:58,244
and it was decided that rather than repair the guest rooms, they'd make a

18
00:00:58,244 --> 00:01:01,890
new house for her out of wood next to the main hall.

19
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:06,087
She stayed there for four nights and apparently it was very luxurious,

20
00:01:06,087 --> 00:01:11,490
but unfortunately it was destroyed a few years later by fire.

21
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:17,376
In the seventeenth century the castle belonged to the wealthy Fenys family,

22
00:01:17,376 --> 00:01:20,590
who enlarged it and made it more comfortable.

23
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:25,791
However, by 1982 the Fenys family could no longer afford to maintain the castle,

24
00:01:25,791 --> 00:01:28,280
even though they received government support,

25
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:30,710
and they put it on the market.

26
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:35,106
It was eventually taken over by a company who owned a number of amusement

27
00:01:35,106 --> 00:01:35,588
parks,

28
00:01:35,588 --> 00:01:39,502
but when we get there I think you'll see that they've managed to retain

29
00:01:39,502 --> 00:01:41,790
the original atmosphere of the castle.

30
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:46,722
When you go inside, you'll find that in the state rooms there are life-like

31
00:01:46,722 --> 00:01:51,754
moving wax models dressed in costumes of different periods in the past,

32
00:01:51,754 --> 00:01:54,400
which even carry on conversations together.

33
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:00,743
As well as that, in every room there are booklets giving information about what the room was used for

34
00:02:00,743 --> 00:02:05,340
and the history of the objects and furniture it contains.

35
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,788
The castle parks quite extensive.

36
00:02:08,788 --> 00:02:10,466
At one time sheep were kept there,

37
00:02:10,466 --> 00:02:16,874
and in the nineteenth century the owners had a little zoo with animals like rabbits

38
00:02:16,874 --> 00:02:19,010
and even a baby elephant.

39
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:25,570
Nowadays the old zoo buildings are used for public displays of paintings and sculpture.

40
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:31,784
The park also has some beautiful trees, though the oldest of all,

41
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:37,480
which dated back 800 years, was sadly blown down in 1987.

42
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:41,657
Now, you're free to wander around on your own until 4:30,

43
00:02:41,657 --> 00:02:46,530
but then at the end of our visit we'll all meet together at the

44
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:48,270
bottom of the Great Staircase.

45
00:02:48,270 --> 00:02:52,952
We'll then go on to the long gallery, where there's a wonderful collection of

46
00:02:52,952 --> 00:02:57,633
photographs showing the family who owned the castle a hundred years ago having tea

47
00:02:57,633 --> 00:03:02,315
and cakes in the conservatory -and we'll then take you to the same place

48
00:03:02,315 --> 00:03:04,990
where afternoon tea will be served to you.

49
00:03:04,990 --> 00:03:10,793
Now if you can take a look at your plans you'll see Branley Castle

50
00:03:10,793 --> 00:03:16,596
has four towers, joined together by a high wall, with the river on two

51
00:03:16,596 --> 00:03:17,010
sides.

52
00:03:17,010 --> 00:03:19,491
Don't miss seeing the Great Hall.

53
00:03:19,491 --> 00:03:24,039
That's near the river in the main tower, the biggest one,

54
00:03:24,039 --> 00:03:27,760
which was extended and redesigned in the eighteenth century.

55
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,991
If you want to get a good view of the whole castle, you can

56
00:03:31,991 --> 00:03:33,200
walk around the walls.

57
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,135
The starting point's quite near the main entrance - walk straight down the path

58
00:03:38,135 --> 00:03:40,792
until you get to the south gate,

59
00:03:40,792 --> 00:03:42,310
and it's just there.

60
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:47,030
Don't go on to the north gate - there's no way up from there.

61
00:03:47,030 --> 00:03:54,161
There'll shortly be a show in which you can see archers displaying their skill with a bow

62
00:03:54,161 --> 00:03:55,000
and arrow.

63
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,581
The quickest way to get there is to take the first left after the main entrance

64
00:04:00,581 --> 00:04:05,465
and follow the path past the bridge, then you'll see it in front of

65
00:04:05,465 --> 00:04:06,860
you at the end.

66
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:12,641
If you like animals there's also a display of hunting birds - falcons

67
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:15,050
and eagles and so on.

68
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:19,837
If you go from the main entrance in the direction of the south gate,

69
00:04:19,837 --> 00:04:24,624
but turn right before you get there instead of going through it, you'll see

70
00:04:24,624 --> 00:04:27,360
it on your right past the first tower.

71
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:34,480
At 3 pm there's a short performance of traditional dancing on the outdoor stage.

72
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:38,898
That's right at the other side of the castle from the entrance,

73
00:04:38,898 --> 00:04:40,370
and over the bridge.

74
00:04:40,370 --> 00:04:43,120
It's about ten minutes' walk or so.

75
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:45,091
And finally the shop.

76
00:04:45,091 --> 00:04:51,987
It's actually inside one of the towers but the way in is from the

77
00:04:51,987 --> 00:04:52,480
outside.

78
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:57,623
Just take the first left after the main entrance, go down the path

79
00:04:57,623 --> 00:04:59,601
and take the first right.

80
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:02,370
It's got some lovely gifts and souvenirs.

81
00:05:02,370 --> 00:05:03,272
Right,

82
00:05:03,272 --> 00:05:06,880
so we're just arriving...
